      "has_children": false,
      "in_trash": false,
      "type": "paragraph",
      "paragraph": {
        "rich_text": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": {
              "content": "This is pretty counterintuitive, right? Like, who cares what already happened, what we should focus on is what can happen next, right?",
              "link": null
            },
            "annotations": {
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false,
              "strikethrough": false,
              "underline": false,
              "code": false,
              "color": "default"
            },
            "plain_text": "This is pretty counterintuitive, right? Like, who cares what already happened, what we should focus on is what can happen next, right?",
            "href": null
          }
        ],
        "icon": null,
        "color": "default"
      }
    },
    {
      "object": "block",
      "id": "731c9e20-f9c9-42e2-a3ea-b8fbd370da0c",
      "parent": {
        "type": "page_id",
        "page_id": "885e5a3e-c419-444b-beb1-6255777c2751"
      },
      "created_time": "2022-06-01T16:32:00.000Z",
      "last_edited_time": "2022-06-01T16:32:00.000Z",
      "created_by": {
        "object": "user",
        "id": "00c350d3-9f7b-4bc1-b27d-24e15ac5139f"
      },
      "last_edited_by": {
        "object": "user",
        "id": "00c350d3-9f7b-4bc1-b27d-24e15ac5139f"
      },
      "has_children": false,
      "in_trash": false,
      "type": "paragraph",
      "paragraph": {
        "rich_text": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": {
              "content": "But spending time on people report what they accomplished provide social feedback, where everyone in the meeting gives kudos in the form of attention for the things that were actually delivered. This means that what we reward is getting work done, as opposed to just talking about work that could happen.",
              "link": null
            },
            "annotations": {
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false,
              "strikethrough": false,
              "underline": false,
              "code": false,
              "color": "default"
            },
            "plain_text": "But spending time on people report what they accomplished provide social feedback, where everyone in the meeting gives kudos in the form of attention for the things that were actually delivered. This means that what we reward is getting work done, as opposed to just talking about work that could happen.",
            "href": null
          }
        ],
        "icon": null,
        "color": "default"
      }
    },
    {
      "object": "block",
      "id": "dd55fffd-5679-4152-965e-9bb515f33b42",
      "parent": {
        "type": "page_id",
        "page_id": "885e5a3e-c419-444b-beb1-6255777c2751"
      },
      "created_time": "2022-06-01T16:32:00.000Z",
      "last_edited_time": "2022-06-01T16:32:00.000Z",
      "created_by": {
        "object": "user",
        "id": "00c350d3-9f7b-4bc1-b27d-24e15ac5139f"
      },
      "last_edited_by": {
        "object": "user",
        "id": "00c350d3-9f7b-4bc1-b27d-24e15ac5139f"
      },
      "has_children": false,
      "in_trash": false,
      "type": "paragraph",
      "paragraph": {
        "rich_text": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": {
              "content": "Another thing: it's really easy to enjoy talking about hypotheticals and what-ifs, but this is actually really dangerous from a motivational perspective! There's a social psychological finding where eg telling your friends \"hey I'm going to write a book\" actually DECREASES your motivation to write a book, because you already get the social kudos up front. I'm almost a believer in keeping my plans secret until they've succeeded, so I don't fall victim to this haha.",
              "link": null
            },
            "annotations": {
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false,
              "strikethrough": false,
              "underline": false,
              "code": false,
              "color": "default"
            },
            "plain_text": "Another thing: it's really easy to enjoy talking about hypotheticals and what-ifs, but this is actually really dangerous from a motivational perspective! There's a social psychological finding where eg telling your friends \"hey I'm going to write a book\" actually DECREASES your motivation to write a book, because you already get the social kudos up front. I'm almost a believer in keeping my plans secret until they've succeeded, so I don't fall victim to this haha.",
            "href": null
          }
        ],
        "icon": null,
        "color": "default"
      }
    },
